Valgrind – Quick README (C++ Memory Debugging Guide)
1. What is Valgrind

Valgrind is a dynamic analysis tool used to detect memory-related problems in programs.

It runs your program inside a virtual CPU and monitors all memory operations.

Most commonly used tool inside Valgrind is Memcheck.

Main purpose:

Detect memory leaks

Detect invalid memory access

Detect double free

Detect use-after-free

Detect uninitialized memory usage

2. Install Valgrind

Linux / WSL:

sudo apt install valgrind

Check installation:

valgrind --version
3. Compile Program Properly

Always compile with debug symbols.

g++ -g program.cpp

Why -g is important:

Shows file name

Shows line number

Shows function where error occurred

Without -g, Valgrind output is harder to read.

4. Basic Command to Run Valgrind
valgrind ./a.out

Recommended command:

valgrind --leak-check=full ./a.out

Meaning:

--leak-check=full → shows detailed memory leak info.

5. Example Program
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    int *ptr = new int[100];
    int *ptr1 = new int[1000];

    ptr1 = ptr;

    delete [] ptr;
}

Memory leak occurs because pointer to new int[1000] is lost.

6. Understanding Valgrind Output

Example output:

HEAP SUMMARY:
    in use at exit: 4000 bytes in 1 blocks
    total heap usage: 4 allocs, 3 frees

LEAK SUMMARY:
    definitely lost: 4000 bytes

Important meanings:

in use at exit

Memory that was not freed before program exit.

total heap usage

Total allocations done by:

your program

C++ runtime

libraries

definitely lost

Memory completely unreachable → real leak

indirectly lost

Memory leaked because another leaked block referenced it.

possibly lost

Pointer may exist but Valgrind can't confirm.

still reachable

Memory still referenced but not freed.

7. Common Errors Valgrind Detects
1️⃣ Memory Leak

Example:

int* p = new int[100];

No delete.

Output:

definitely lost: 400 bytes
2️⃣ Invalid Read

Example:

int arr[5];
arr[10] = 5;

Output:

Invalid write of size 4
3️⃣ Use After Free

Example:

int* p = new int;
delete p;
*p = 10;

Output:

Invalid write of size 4
4️⃣ Double Free

Example:

delete p;
delete p;

Output:

Invalid free()
8. Important Valgrind Options
Show detailed leaks
valgrind --leak-check=full ./a.out
Show origins of uninitialized values
valgrind --track-origins=yes ./a.out
Show full stack trace
valgrind --leak-check=full --show-leak-kinds=all ./a.out
9. Why Valgrind Shows More Memory Than Your Code

Valgrind counts allocations from:

C++ runtime

iostream

glibc

system libraries

Example:

Your program:

4400 bytes

Valgrind may show:

79,000 bytes allocated

Because it includes library allocations.

Focus on:

definitely lost

This is your actual leak.

10. Performance Impact

Valgrind slows programs significantly.

Typical slowdown:

10x – 30x slower

Because every memory access is monitored.

11. When to Use Valgrind

Use Valgrind when:

Debugging memory leaks

Debugging segmentation faults

Debugging invalid memory access

Debugging heap corruption

Especially useful in:

C

C++

embedded systems

systems programming

12. Valgrind vs AddressSanitizer
Feature	Valgrind	AddressSanitizer
Speed	slow	fast
Requires recompilation	no	yes
Memory errors detection	excellent	excellent
Used in CI pipelines	less common	very common

Typical workflow:

Development → AddressSanitizer
Deep debugging → Valgrind
13. Best Practice to Avoid Memory Leaks

Prefer RAII containers instead of raw pointers.

Good:

vector<int> v(100);

Bad:

int* p = new int[100];

Even better:

smart pointers

Example:

unique_ptr<int[]> p(new int[100]);
14. Professional Debugging Workflow

Typical workflow for C++ engineers:

compile with -g
run program with AddressSanitizer
run tests
run Valgrind for leak detection
fix memory errors
15. Key Takeaways

Valgrind detects runtime memory errors.

Use --leak-check=full for detailed reports.

Focus on definitely lost memory.

Compile with -g for useful debugging information.

Expect extra allocations from libraries.